---
import { t, UI_LOCALES } from '../i18n/index.js';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/prose.css';

const repo = 'https://github.com/eckberg/fridgeweek';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={t('en', 'meta.aboutTitle')}
  description={t('en', 'meta.aboutDescription')}
  current="about"
>
  <article class="page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>{t('en', 'about.title')}</h1>

    <h2 id="what">{t('en', 'about.whatTitle')}</h2>
    <p>
      Fridgeweek makes one thing: a print-ready sheet with a week on it, seven days from top to
      bottom, all the same height. You set your household up once, print a stack, and write the
      week on paper with a pen. It was built for a household that was already drawing this sheet
      by hand every Sunday and wanted to stop redrawing the skeleton.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is deliberately small. These are the things it will not do, and they are choices rather
      than a roadmap:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>No calendar sync, no accounts, no server-side storage of any kind.</li>
      <li>
        Nothing is pre-printed except dates. The sheet is a skeleton; its value is that it stays
        paper.
      </li>
      <li>No screen view, no app, no recurring events.</li>
      <li>
        No poster aesthetics. The sheet is plain, dense and functional, printed in black on
        whatever paper you have.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The interface is available in {UI_LOCALES.length} languages, and the weekday names, date
      formats and first day of the week come from your browser's own locale data, so a great many
      more languages print correctly on the sheet itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="privacy">{t('en', 'about.privacyTitle')}</h2>
    <p>
      There is no account, no database and no analytics on this site. No cookies are set. Nothing
      you type is recorded anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your sheet's configuration lives in two places, both of them yours: the address bar, which
      is what makes a sheet a shareable link, and your browser's local storage, so that coming
      back to the bare address restores your household. Clearing your browser data removes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The one exception is the PDF button. If a deployment has a PDF renderer, pressing it sends
      your configuration &mdash; the names, symbols and settings, nothing else &mdash; to that
      renderer, which draws the sheet, returns the file and keeps nothing. Printing from your
      browser avoids even that, and every browser can save a printout as a PDF.
    </p>

    <h2 id="terms">{t('en', 'about.termsTitle')}</h2>
    <p>
      Fridgeweek is provided as is, without warranty of any kind. It is a tool for printing a
      blank sheet of paper; please do not rely on it for anything that matters more than that.
      Use of the software is governed by the MIT licence below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="licence">{t('en', 'about.licenceTitle')}</h2>
    <p>
      The code is licensed under the MIT licence and the whole project is on GitHub at{' '}
      <a href={repo} rel="noopener">eckberg/fridgeweek</a>. You are free to read it, run your own
      copy, change it, or fold it into something else.
    </p>
    <p>Two bundled works keep their own licences:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Atkinson Hyperlegible Next</strong>, the typeface, under the SIL Open Font
        License 1.1. It was designed for maximum distinction between letterforms, which is why a
        sheet meant for children learning to read uses it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lucide</strong>, the icon set the symbols come from, under the ISC licence. A few
        symbols are drawn for this project and are MIT like the rest of the code.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Neither licence puts any attribution requirement on a sheet you print. The full notices are
      in <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`} rel="noopener">THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contributing">Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Bug reports and fixes are welcome, and the two contributions most wanted are a new language
      and a new symbol. Both have a step-by-step recipe in{' '}
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`} rel="noopener">CONTRIBUTING.md</a>, and
      neither asks you to understand the layout engine.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-5) var(--space-9);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .page {
      padding: var(--space-7) var(--space-4) var(--space-8);
    }
  }
</style>
